---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You'll move matter back and forth across the line between physical and
    digital, as many times as a piece needs: start from an object or start
    from code, and cross that line in whichever direction it isn't currently
    on — digitizing (scanning, photogrammetry, projection), computing
    (creative coding, state machines, rule systems), and physical-computing
    or fabrication (Arduino sensors and actuators, camera-based tracking, 3D
    printing and laser cutting) are three techniques you can chain in
    whatever order and for however many trips a piece asks for, ending
    wherever it makes sense to end — as a digital artifact, a physical one,
    or something that's genuinely both. Two studio briefs and a final
    project ask you to combine techniques into one working piece rather than
    treat them as separate exercises, and you'll leave with a small personal
    toolkit spanning both halves — plus a finished piece shown at the
    semester's grand show. The <a href={withBase("/gallery/")}>Gallery</a> collects
    examples of what these techniques can build: some are complete pieces on
    their own, others are single-technique components meant to be combined
    into something that fits the final project's brief.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Third-year students with real depth in <em>either</em> physical or
    studio-art practice <em>or</em> programming — not both. The course
    cross-trains whichever half you don't already have, using your existing
    strength as the foundation, and studio pairs are built to make sure of
    it: you're matched with someone whose strength is the other half of
    yours. It runs as a studio and crit sequence, with work shown to an
    audience most weeks, not as lectures with a written exam.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>
        One four-hour studio afternoon a week — lecture then tutorial some
        weeks, hands-on throughout in others. What's happening, and this
        week's runsheet.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Gallery" href="/gallery/">
      <p>What the semester's techniques can build, drawn from every lecture's deck.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
